India's 52,000 crore rupee FBI sell-off this month shows why TFSA holders need tighter country diversification rules. This is Modern Investing Techniques, Episode 204. Quick reminder, everything we discuss here is for education and entertainment. The practice investment of the day uses simulated trades with no real money. I'm not a licensed financial advisor and this isn't financial advice. Always do your own research before putting real money to work. Foreign portfolio investors, or FBI,

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.640
AI, kept selling Indian equities even as the Nifty index held steady, according to

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.680
the economic times.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:41.500
Canadian investors sitting in emerging market exchange traded funds, or ETFs, should review

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:45.260
those country weights inside a tax-free savings account, or TFSA.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.860
The rule I use is a 15% cap of TFSA equity on any single non-North American allocation.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:56.800
Concentrated foreign selling can run that hard without the local index taking an immediate

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:57.120
hit.

